WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.509
In 1972 the chess world witnessed something
very special. It was this Round 6 game of

00:00:12.509 --> 00:00:17.240
the World Chess Championship between Bobby
Fischer and Boris Spassky. Special for many

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:23.339
reasons. To name a few...Fischer for only
the third time in serious play began with

00:00:23.339 --> 00:00:29.560
c4, and regarding the game itself it's maybe
best described in the words of International

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:36.560
Master Anthony Saidy who said, "It was like
a symphony of placid beauty." And not to be

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:44.180
overlooked was a wonderful act of sportsmanship
by Boris Spassky who, after the game, stood

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:51.070
up and applauded Fischer for the masterful
game he just played. This is that game. So

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:58.070
Fischer opening up with c4, the English Opening.
But what we'll soon see is that it transposes

00:00:58.129 --> 00:01:04.580
into a Queen's Gambit Declined. And that can
be denoted by these group of pawns right here.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:11.580
After knight f6, knight c3, soon enough we
enter the Tartakower variation with this move

00:01:12.729 --> 00:01:19.300
seven, b6. This h6 move is just throwing a
question to the bishop. There's not really

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:24.060
going to be any way to exploit this type of
a weakness against the black kingside. It's

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:30.649
more longterm flight square for that king.
And you'll note that after this b6 advance...it's

00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:37.649
only at this point does Fischer decide to
release the tension between these two pawns.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:45.560
Only after black has spent a move in creating
a new door for this bishop. If white is to

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:53.039
capture maybe prior to that b6 move, then
the bishop can make use of this classic diagonal.

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:59.069
But again, only after that b6 advance does
white now clarify the structure in the center,

00:01:59.069 --> 00:02:06.069
capturing on d5. We do have a series of exchanges.
And after the smoke clears, this is the pawn

00:02:06.709 --> 00:02:13.709
structure we're now dealt with. Rook c1, logical
enough applying pressure to this c7 pawn.

00:02:14.349 --> 00:02:21.349
Bishop e6, a curious move. One may think why
not just play to b7? b6 was played for a purpose

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:28.750
but black is going forward with the bishop
e6 move. If you do a quick comparison between

00:02:29.379 --> 00:02:36.379
this e6 square and b7 square...some of the
differences may include well from b7 it's

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:42.749
unprotected...e6 it's not. And additionally
the bishop might want to later make use of

00:02:42.749 --> 00:02:49.749
let's say f5, or even harass the knight by
playing to g4. So now queen a4, and one of

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:57.620
the interesting things in my opinion with
this game is how the white queen makes use

00:02:58.409 --> 00:03:04.810
of the whole chessboard. Or really, maybe
better stated, wherever she is that's where

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:11.090
the ensuing play will take place. So right
now stuff is happening on the queenside after

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:18.069
this queen a4 move. Again still looking to
do something against this c7 pawn. Black presses

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:24.120
forward challenging that d4 point of white's.
Queen a3, this is a bit of an uncomfortable

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:31.120
pin. A third piece is now hitting that c5
pawn...rook, pawn, and queen. It needs additional

00:03:32.579 --> 00:03:38.769
support. Rook c8 is decided on, and now more
development by white. One step closer to being

00:03:38.769 --> 00:03:45.279
fully developed, and why not play to b5. In
some sense you're just boxing out that knight

00:03:45.279 --> 00:03:52.279
on b8. a6 is, for the moment, not yet a threat
since this rook is unprotected. So there's

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:58.090
no need to react to a threat that's not there.
Instead we just have more clarification in

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:05.090
the center with d takes c, b takes c, and
the resulting structure is the hanging pawn

00:04:05.669 --> 00:04:10.760
structure. So ideally white would like to
have both rooks on the c and d-file applying

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:17.100
pressure to these guys. White castles, fully
development. And now only after rook a7, and

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:22.970
the queen now defending that rook does white
react with bishop to e2 just tucking it away.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:29.970
And just taking a snapshot of this position
right here...it just seems so smooth right

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:38.230
now. The knight on a classic square f3, the
bishop tucked away on e2, a rook very well

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:45.250
placed, soon enough the f-rook one would think
is going to be placed to the d1 square, black

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:51.410
being still in this slightly awkward pin...okay
yes it's protected right here. But on the

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:57.280
black end, look at the disjointed nature between
these rooks right here, and the knight still

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:03.260
on its home square...an isolated pawn on a6.
After just these seventeen moves, okay it's

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:10.260
a pretty decent amount of moves but, the differences
between both sides I think is a great one.

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:16.840
And soon enough what we'll see is an additional
imbalance taking place. As it stands right

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:23.840
now, it's just with regard to the pawn structure...this
hanging pawn structure. So more development

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:32.130
for black, fully developed at this point,
but again not so harmonious. Knight d4, making

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:38.210
use of this pin. This knight is now providing
a disconnect between the queen and rook. So

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:45.210
a very timely knight d4 is going to grant
this knight for bishop exchange. So the queen

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:51.080
backs up, getting out of the pin and just
for the one moment...well just taking that

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:57.630
bishop right away. And after the recapture
we have now one strike in the center that

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:03.810
really is going to turn the tables. These
pawns right here...the furthest advanced ones...this

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:10.810
pawn duo on the fifth rank is challenged with
e4, and something needs to be done. And what

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:17.610
black tries is just pressing forward which
is typical. If you do have the knight you

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:24.030
want to try and keep things closed. That d4
move is a step in that direction. It's also

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:30.220
a passed pawn. But as we'll soon see, this
pawn really could just never get going and

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:37.220
is no serious threat to white whatsoever.
Additionally with that advance, we now...looking

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:44.550
a bit more closely at the pawn structure...recognize
that we have a four on three majority tipping

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:52.090
in white's favor on the kingside. Now that
said, that's not to say you're supposed to

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:59.090
throw up all of your pawns in this direction.
It's more commonly seen that just the f-pawn

00:07:01.310 --> 00:07:07.780
and e-pawn for white will be the ones to press
forward and try and induce some weakness against

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:14.210
the black side. And the g and h-pawns would
just stay back and act as the shield for that

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:20.080
white king. So that's exactly what we see.
f4, these pawns get rolling, queen e7, and

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:27.080
now e5. Right here this now a static point
for black, this pawn is immobile and will

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:34.410
soon be pinpointed. So rook to b8, it wasn't
really needed anymore to watch over that c5

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:40.860
square...so now coming back to this b-file
to exert pressure on b2. Bishop c4...repositioning

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:47.000
time at this point placing this pawn in a
pin and looking forward to an f5 advance where

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:52.870
this pawn is just going to strike at e6. Black
gets out of that, and now the queen swinging

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:59.390
over. Again, really dictating where the play
will take place in the game. So making use

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:06.390
of this whole third rank right here...sweeping
from a3 to h3 and now pinpointing that e6

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:13.400
pawn for a second time. Knight f8, retreat
to defend e6. And now something as simple

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:20.400
as b3 makes these rooks look absolutely ridiculous.
Because there's nothing that's now going to

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:28.290
take place on the queenside. You could forget
about it. Just this bishop and two pawns alone...this

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:35.200
is one thing that I like to do in my own games
is just recognize how a particular grouping

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:42.200
of pieces can negate an opponent's group of
pieces. And right now it's just these three

00:08:43.339 --> 00:08:48.240
highlighted white pieces negating these three
right here. Or in other words you could view

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:55.240
it as like a five points negating eleven points.
And on the flip side of things there's going

00:08:56.930 --> 00:09:03.930
to now be a group of let's say these two rooks
and queen, and even the bishop that are influencing

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:10.120
the kingside. This bishop right here really
being a multipurpose pieces in some sense...locking

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:17.120
down on the queenside, and still exerting
pressure on that kingside. So a5, looking

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:24.579
to get in this advance right here...looking
to maybe peel open that a-file. But things

00:09:24.579 --> 00:09:30.220
happen too quickly on the kingside. f5, something
needing to be done about maybe capturing on

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:36.439
e6 or even pressing forward one more step
to f6, and that king will be in some serious

00:09:36.439 --> 00:09:42.269
hot water if that's allowed. So the pawn is
captured, and it's the rook on f that recaptures

00:09:42.269 --> 00:09:49.269
so that this rook can soon contribute...doubling
on the f-file. Knight h7, looking to reposition

00:09:50.279 --> 00:09:57.279
to that g5 square. White doubles, the queen
backs up to d8. Queen g3 allows this pawn

00:09:57.689 --> 00:10:04.689
to press forward to h4. And what we basically
see now is that move played. And look at this

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:10.379
sad knight right here. It's just completely
restricted, and this bishop is incredibly

00:10:10.379 --> 00:10:16.180
dominant. The differences between this bishop
and knight are just enormous. This bishop

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:23.180
is just way too powerful, as we will soon
see in the resulting final position. So there's

00:10:24.319 --> 00:10:30.870
not really a whole lot unfortunately for black
to be done right here. These rooks are just

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:37.809
helpless to really doing anything. This is
the only completely open file, and white controls

00:10:37.809 --> 00:10:44.809
it. It really cannot be contested whatsoever.
So pressing forward with e6, rook c7 defending

00:10:46.459 --> 00:10:53.459
this c5 pawn. Queen e5, repositioning for
white . And unfortunately for black...for

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:58.829
pretty much the duration of the game...just
can do nothing more that just shuffle the

00:10:58.829 --> 00:11:05.829
queen on these d8 and e8 squares. So basically
emphasizing at this point is white...you know

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:14.839
what black has nothing to do. Just a4 doesn't
even allow the possibility for black to play

00:11:14.839 --> 00:11:21.839
a4 and create some opening on the queenside.
So queen d8, rook f2...a slightly, I guess,

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:27.220
curious move. Maybe in some sense it's saying...
you know what...there's nothing that you could

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:34.220
even do. Maybe just emphasizing the fact that
black has again nothing. Or maybe also another

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:42.360
way to view it is it's wanting to play to
f3 so that after this queen d8 move...white

00:11:43.439 --> 00:11:49.980
has bishop d3 and this pawn can now not be
taken. White maybe wanting this particular

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:56.579
sequence to come about so that bishop d3 could
be played and there's not going to now be

00:11:56.579 --> 00:12:01.240
rook takes pawn if the queen is still on e8.
This would now be available after the bishop

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:08.240
d3 move. But as it stands after queen d8,
white now focuses the attention on this diagonal

00:12:08.619 --> 00:12:15.619
right here, and soon enough we have a tactical
shot coming into play here. Nice battery...the

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:22.350
threat now being rook to f8, which is going
to be deadly. For example, if rook takes e6

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:27.899
we would just have rook check, knight takes
rook, rook takes knight removing the defender

00:12:27.899 --> 00:12:34.899
of h7, queen takes rook, queen h7 and that's
game over. So rook takes pawn is not played.

00:12:36.119 --> 00:12:41.300
Instead what is tried is just a knight move
to f6, but Fischer just rips the position

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:48.300
right open...grabbing that knight, recapture
a couple times on f6, and king to g8. This

00:12:48.329 --> 00:12:54.550
is a hopeless position for black. What is
tried is just king g8. But after bishop c4,

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:59.559
the king is just having to get off of this
diagonal so that this rook maybe has the possibility

00:12:59.559 --> 00:13:05.459
to move. Moving it now is going to allow the
pawn to just press forward...one step closer

00:13:05.459 --> 00:13:10.420
to queening as well as having a discovered
check against the king. So the king goes back

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:17.009
to the corner. But after queen to f4, black
can do nothing more than resign right here.

00:13:17.009 --> 00:13:21.509
The threats are just way too much. We have
possibilities for rook to f8, winning the

00:13:21.509 --> 00:13:28.509
queen...rook takes h6 check...queen takes
h6 check. There's not really a whole lot black

00:13:29.869 --> 00:13:36.329
can do. Just to show one variation...if the
king is to let's say come back over to g8,

00:13:36.329 --> 00:13:43.329
we could have queen takes h6. And with now
the threat of rook g6 check, and pawn push...there's

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:48.559
not really a whole lot black can do. Just
kind of making a passing move...if rook b7,

00:13:48.559 --> 00:13:55.559
we could have this check, rook block, pawn
push discovered check. And what to do? The

00:13:56.629 --> 00:14:00.720
queen has to block. You could pick your favorite
way to mate at this point...promotion or just

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:07.720
capturing right here. However you slice it,
black is beat from this position after Fischer's

00:14:08.819 --> 00:14:15.819
41st move, queen to f4. So, in this video,
it's really my hope that it acts as a great

00:14:19.079 --> 00:14:25.459
example of how the whole chessboard can be
made use of...and how seemingly simple moves

00:14:25.459 --> 00:14:32.459
can translate into a significant advantage...and
of how competitors of this great game can,

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:39.649
or really should carry themselves in a similar
way as Boris Spassky did in defeat.

